  "correctness_argument": "Borchert 2023 (#913) releases, for 64,853 (gene, comparison) rows over 13 comparison sheets of si1.xlsx (sha256 b80c6866...), a two-sample t test of a gene's RB-TnSeq fitness between a condition arm and a reference arm: 't-statistic', 'p-value', 'q-value' (pFDR) and 'adjusted_q-value' (monotonicity-adjusted), 259,412 values that no class could hold. Measured against #880's fields first, as the issue asks: EnvironmentResponsePhenotype carries a per-culture response with a t statistic, an unadjusted p-value and ONE adjusted p-value. A contrast record there would need environment_response set (its t-statistic validator requires the response it tests), and the release states no contrast value (only each arm's mean, from which a difference would be our arithmetic); it has no slot for a second q-value; and its t and p are documented as a test of ONE culture's response, so filing a two-arm test there changes what those fields mean (Rule 3, field_meaning). So a sibling leaf (Rule 6: a released quantity that differs in meaning from an existing field is a sibling leaf even on the same technology): FitnessContrastPhenotype under Phenotype, beside EnvironmentResponsePhenotype, which keeps holding each arm's per-culture fitness. Its own experiment pair, BacterialFitnessContrastExperiment / ...Reference (assembly-pinned), puts the condition arm in the record's environment and the reference arm in the reference's. Every statistic is value-or-gap (a reference arm tests nothing, so its statistics are typed gaps, never invented null values), stored verbatim: a pFDR q-value is not bounded above (582 released q-values exceed 1), p is a probability, a q-value names its correction. Additive: no served record serializes differently; the four bindings gain one member each.",
  "measurement_type_decision": true,
  "source_quotes": [
    {
      "value": "two-sample t test, pFDR q values",
      "provenance": {
        "source_uri": "paper.md",
        "citation_key": "borchertRBTnSeqIdentifiesGenetic2023",
        "sha256": "e156d3b2139add2feb8b3ffb1b319f9a7d369d6ee9b6454f67c38d33c924265a",
        "method": null,
        "page": null,
        "retrieved": null
      },
      "quote": "Comparison of mean fitness values between enrichment and medium reference cultures $( \\mathbf { M 9 } + 2 0 \\mathbf { m M }$ glucose alone) was performed using a two-sample $t$ test, where the $p$ value was corrected for multiple testing via the positive false discovery rate (pFDR) method",
      "note": null
    },
    {
      "value": "monotonicity-adjusted pFDR q",
      "provenance": {
        "source_uri": "paper.md",
        "citation_key": "borchertRBTnSeqIdentifiesGenetic2023",
        "sha256": "e156d3b2139add2feb8b3ffb1b319f9a7d369d6ee9b6454f67c38d33c924265a",
        "method": null,
        "page": null,
        "retrieved": null
      },
      "quote": "pFDR $q$ values were then adjusted for monotonicity (Yekutieli and Benjamini, 1999), and both unadjusted and adjusted $q$ values are reported.",
      "note": null
    }
  ],
  "alternatives_rejected": [
    {
      "alternative": "Store the contrast on EnvironmentResponsePhenotype with #880's p-value pair",
      "why_rejected": "Needs a released contrast value it does not have, has one adjusted p-value for two released q-values, and redefines a per-culture test as a two-arm test (field_meaning)."
    },
    {
      "alternative": "Attach the statistics to one of the two arms' per-culture records",
      "why_rejected": "Misfiles a statistic of the difference on one arm (the original refusal in the Borchert 2023 note)."
    },
    {
      "alternative": "Keep refusing the 259,412 values",
      "why_rejected": "Owner rule 2026-10-12: every released value is stored."
    }
  ],
